; A bounded channel of words between `parallel` bindings (R-C2,
; docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-10).
;
; WHAT IT IS. A ring of `cap` machine words in a MAP_SHARED mapping,
; with a lock and an event counter beside it. Any number of bindings may
; send and receive; `chanSend` blocks while the ring is full and
; `chanRecv` while it is empty; `chanClose` ends the stream, after which
; a send is refused and a receive drains what is left and then answers
; `None`. Values leave in the order they were sent (one total order over
; all senders, FIFO within each).
;
; WHY A MAPPING, AND WHY BOTH LOWERINGS. A binding runs in a forked child
; by default and in a thread under `--threads`. An arena page is private
; after a fork - a copy - so a ring allocated there would be two rings.
; A mapping made with `sysMapShared` BEFORE the `parallel` form is the
; same pages in the parent and in every child, and in every thread, so
; one channel serves both lowerings without the program choosing.
;
; WHAT CROSSES IS A WORD. An `Int`. A heap value cannot: a forked child
; has its own arena, and a thread's arena is unmapped when the thread
; ends (MM-PAR-6a), so a handle sent through here would name memory the
; receiver does not own. Typed transfer of heap values is R-C2's open
; half.
;
; THE HANDLE IS A `Chan` (docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-8): a word struct
; (MM-VAL-10a) that only this module can make or read, so no other code
; can hand a channel operation a word `chanNew` did not answer. Its word
; is a slot in the runtime's handle table, kind `chanKind`, naming the
; mapping, and every public function asks the table for the mapping
; first (`chanAt`). So an operation on a freed channel, and a second
; `chanFree`, trap with status 85 instead of touching the unmapped ring.
; It is declared `shared`, because every operation below is built for
; use from every binding at once, so a `parallel` binding may capture it
; in either lowering (AX3064 admits it). What stays the program's: free
; a channel only once no binding can still reach it - after the
; `parallel` form that used it. A free that RACES another binding's
; operation is a data race (MM-PAR-9): the check covers every operation
; ordered after the free, not one already in flight.
;
; HOW IT BLOCKS. Word 0 is a three-state lock (0 free, 1 held, 2 held
; with waiters - Drepper's futex mutex): an uncontended acquire and
; release are one compare-and-swap and one add, and no syscall. Word 1
; counts every change a waiter could be waiting for. A binding that must
; wait reads the counter under the lock, announces itself in word 2,
; releases, and sleeps in `sysWaitWord` while the counter still holds
; what it read - so a change made between its release and its sleep is
; seen on entry, and no wake is lost. A changer bumps the counter under
; the lock and wakes only if word 2 says someone waits. Where the target
; has no blocking wait (`waitWordKind` 0: FreeBSD for now) the same code
; spins: correct, and it costs a core.
;
; TIMED FORMS (MM-PAR-12). `chanSendTimeout` and
; `chanRecvTimeout` wait at most a given number of nanoseconds and then
; answer `Err` with code `sysTimedOut`, having sent or taken nothing.
; The wait is `sysWaitWordTimeout` on the same counter, in slices; a
; slice the kernel timed out costs the whole slice and one cut short
; costs the clock's step, clamped to it, so a wait nobody ends is never
; shorter than asked. The ring is looked at once more after the last
; slice: a word that arrives as the time runs out is taken.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVIDE. No timeout on `chanSend` and `chanRecv`
; themselves (a receive on a channel nobody closes waits forever, and a
; thread cannot be killed out of it - MM-PAR-7's sweep JOINS threads;
; the timed forms above are the way out of a wait nobody ends, though
; not of a lock a dead binding holds, which they take like every other
; call); no fairness between waiters (a wake
; wakes all, and whoever takes the lock first wins); no priority
; inheritance; not callable from a signal handler (the lock is not
; reentrant). And no survival of a binding that dies holding the lock:
; MM-PAR-7's sweep SIGKILLs a forked sibling wherever it is, so one
; killed between `chanLock` and `chanUnlock` leaves word 0 held for good,
; and the next call on the channel blocks forever (an independent review
; measured it: 6 of 10 runs of a trap-and-recover probe hung). After a
; sweep - a recovered trap, an abort - the only safe call on a channel
; the swept bindings used is `chanFree`, which takes no lock.
; Words 3-7: head, tail, capacity, closed, mapping length.
(import Mem)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; ---- the handle ------------------------------------------------------
; A channel: one word, a slot in the runtime's handle table (MM-PAR-8)
; naming the ring's mapping.
(pub struct Chan word shared
  (slot : Int))

; The handle table's kind for a channel. The table refuses a word of any
; other kind, so a mutex's handle is not a channel even to code that
; forged one.
(:: chanKind Int)
(fn (chanKind)
  2)

; The ring a live channel names, or the 85 trap for one that was freed.
(:: chanAt (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanAt c)
  (__handle_get chanKind c.slot))

; A handle for a fresh ring, or 0 when the table has no slot left.
(:: chanName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanName ch)
  (__handle_new chanKind ch))

; Retire a live channel's handle and answer its ring, or the 85 trap for
; a channel already freed.
(:: chanRetire (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanRetire c)
  (__handle_free chanKind c.slot))

; ---- the lock --------------------------------------------------------
; Set the word at `a` to `v` and answer what it held: a compare-and-swap
; loop, because the language's atomics have no exchange.
(:: chanXchg (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanXchg a v)
  (let (
    (mut old (__atomic_load a))
    (mut done 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== done 0)
        (let ((found (__atomic_cas a old v)))
          (if (== found old)
            (set done 1)
            (set old found))))
      old
    }))

(:: chanLock (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanLock ch)
  (if (== (__atomic_cas ch 0 1) 0)
    0
    (let ((mut c (chanXchg ch 2)))
      {
        (while (!= c 0)
          {
            (sysWaitWord ch 2)
            (set c (chanXchg ch 2))
          })
        0
      })))

; 1 -> 0 is the uncontended release. 2 -> 1 means someone waits: clear
; it and wake them.
(:: chanUnlock (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanUnlock ch)
  (if (== (__atomic_add ch (- 0 1)) 1)
    0
    {
      (__atomic_store ch 0)
      (sysWakeWord ch)
      0
    }))

; ---- waiting for a change ---------------------------------------------
; Under the lock: announce a waiter and answer the counter it must wait
; on. The caller releases, calls `chanSleep`, and retries.
(:: chanPark (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanPark ch)
  {
    (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) 1)
    (__atomic_load (+ ch 8))
  })

(:: chanSleep (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanSleep ch seen)
  {
    (sysWaitWord (+ ch 8) seen)
    (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) (- 0 1))
    0
  })

; `chanSleep` for at most `nanos`: `sysWaitWordTimeout`'s answer - 1
; when the kernel measured the whole of it and nobody woke this waiter.
(:: chanSleepFor (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanSleepFor ch seen nanos)
  (let ((code (sysWaitWordTimeout (+ ch 8) seen nanos)))
    {
      (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) (- 0 1))
      code
    }))

; ---- time left ---------------------------------------------------------
; A timed call keeps two words in a 32-byte scratch block: word 2 the
; nanoseconds it has left, word 3 the clock reading it last took (words
; 0-1 are the clock's own buffer). `chanTimeSpent` charges one wait to
; it: the whole slice when the kernel measured it (`code` 1), otherwise
; the clock's step, clamped to [0, slice] - so a step of Darwin's
; realtime clock (`sysTimeoutMicros`) moves the wait by at most the one
; slice it landed in, and a wait nobody wakes is never shorter than
; asked, on any target. `docs/memory-model.md` MM-PAR-12.
(:: chanScratch (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanScratch nanos)
  (let ((b (memAlloc 32)))
    {
      (__retain b)
      (__store64 b 2 nanos)
      (__store64 b 3 (sysTimeoutMicros b))
      b
    }))

(:: chanScratchDone (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:precondition(`b` is a block `chanScratch` answered, handed back once and not used after)
(fn (chanScratchDone b)
  {
    (__release b)
    0
  })

(:: chanTimeLeft (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanTimeLeft b)
  (__load64 b 2))

(:: chanTimeSpent (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanTimeSpent b code slice)
  (let (
    (left (__load64 b 2))
    (now (sysTimeoutMicros b))
  )
    (let ((step (chanStep code slice (* (- now (__load64 b 3)) 1000))))
      {
        (__store64 b 2 (- left step))
        (__store64 b 3 now)
        0
      })))

; How long the next wait may be: the time left, or `chanSliceNanos` if
; that is shorter. A slice bounds what one clock step can cost: with the
; whole remaining time as ONE slice, a woken wait charged a forward step
; of Darwin's realtime clock could end the entire wait early.
(:: chanSlice (-> Int Int))
(fn (chanSlice b)
  (if (< (chanTimeLeft b) chanSliceNanos)
    (chanTimeLeft b)
    chanSliceNanos))

; The longest one timed wait sleeps before it re-reads the clock: 100 ms,
; `Sync.ax`'s probe interval.
(:: chanSliceNanos Int)
(fn (chanSliceNanos)
  100000000)

; The nanoseconds one wait costs: all of `slice` when the kernel timed
; it out, otherwise the clock's step `d` clamped to [0, slice].
(:: chanStep (-> Int Int Int Int))
(fn (chanStep code slice d)
  (if (== code 1)
    slice
    (if (< d 0)
      0
      (if (> d slice)
        slice
        d))))

; Under the lock: a change a waiter may be waiting for.
(:: chanBump (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanBump ch)
  (__atomic_add (+ ch 8) 1))

; After the release: wake the waiters, if the word says there are any.
(:: chanNotify (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanNotify ch)
  (if (> (__atomic_load (+ ch 16)) 0)
    (sysWakeWord (+ ch 8))
    0))

; ---- the words -------------------------------------------------------
; Word `i` of the channel, and a store to it. Plain accesses, made only
; under the lock, whose compare-and-swap and add order them (MM-PAR-9).
; The two are the channel's only raw loads and stores; every public
; function below goes through them and claims no more than `effect(io)`
; (`chanCap`, which performs no IO, claims nothing), which is
; what keeps this module a safe interface in MM-PAR-9's reading.
(:: chanGet (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanGet ch i)
  (__load64 ch i))

(:: chanPut (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanPut ch i v)
  (__store64 ch i v))

; ---- the channel ------------------------------------------------------
; A channel of `cap` words, 1 <= cap <= 1,048,576. Answers the handle,
; or the mapping's error; a capacity out of range is EINVAL (22 on every
; target with a syscall ABI), and a handle table with no slot left is
; EMFILE (24).
(pub :: chanNew (-> Int (Result Chan Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanNew cap)
  (if (|| (< cap 1) (> cap 1048576))
    (Err (mkError 22 "chanNew: capacity must be 1..1048576"))
    (let ((len (* 8 (+ cap 8))))
      (match (sysMapShared len)
        ((Ok ch)
          {
            (chanPut ch 5 cap)
            (chanPut ch 7 len)
            (chanNamed ch len)
          }
        )
        ((Err e) (Err e))))))

; Name a fresh ring in the handle table, or unmap it and say why not.
(:: chanNamed (-> Int Int (Result Chan Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (chanNamed ch len)
  (let ((h (chanName ch)))
    (if (== h 0)
      (match (sysUnmapShared ch len)
        ((Ok z) (Err (mkError 24 "chanNew: every handle is in use")))
        ((Err e) (Err (mkError 24 "chanNew: every handle is in use"))))
      (Ok (Chan h)))))

; Send `v`, waiting while the ring is full. `True` once it is in the
; ring; `False` if the channel is closed - before the call or while it
; waited - and then `v` was not sent.
(pub :: chanSend (-> Chan Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanSend c v)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (mut state 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        {
          (chanLock ch)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
              {
                (chanUnlock ch)
                (set state 2)
              }
              (if (< (- tail head) cap)
                {
                  (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
                  (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
                  (chanBump ch)
                  (chanUnlock ch)
                  (chanNotify ch)
                  (set state 1)
                }
                (let ((seen (chanPark ch)))
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch)
                    (chanSleep ch seen)
                  }))))
        })
      (== state 1)
    }))

; Receive the oldest word, waiting while the ring is empty and open.
; `None` once the channel is closed AND drained - the end of the stream.
(pub :: chanRecv (-> Chan (Option Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanRecv c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (mut state 0)
    (mut got 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        {
          (chanLock ch)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (< head tail)
              {
                (set got (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap))))
                (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
                (chanBump ch)
                (chanUnlock ch)
                (chanNotify ch)
                (set state 1)
              }
              (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
                {
                  (chanUnlock ch)
                  (set state 2)
                }
                (let ((seen (chanPark ch)))
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch)
                    (chanSleep ch seen)
                  }))))
        })
      (if (== state 1)
        (Some got)
        None)
    }))

; `chanSend`, waiting at most `nanos` nanoseconds for room. `Ok True`
; once `v` is in the ring; `Ok False` if the channel is closed; `Err`
; with code `sysTimedOut` when the time ran out with the ring still
; full - and then `v` was not sent. The ring is looked at once more
; after the last wait, so a slot that opened as the time ran out is
; taken rather than refused. A non-positive `nanos` is one look, like
; `chanTrySend`, that says which of the three it was.
(pub :: chanSendTimeout (-> Chan Int Int (Result Bool Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (chanSendTimeout c v nanos)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (b (chanScratch nanos))
    (mut state 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        {
          (chanLock ch)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
              {
                (chanUnlock ch)
                (set state 2)
              }
              (if (< (- tail head) cap)
                {
                  (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
                  (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
                  (chanBump ch)
                  (chanUnlock ch)
                  (chanNotify ch)
                  (set state 1)
                }
                (if (<= (chanTimeLeft b) 0)
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch)
                    (set state 3)
                  }
                  (let (
                    (seen (chanPark ch))
                    (slice (chanSlice b))
                  )
                    {
                      (chanUnlock ch)
                      (chanTimeSpent b (chanSleepFor ch seen slice) slice)
                    })))))
        })
      (chanScratchDone b)
      (if (== state 3)
        (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "chanSendTimeout: timed out"))
        (Ok (== state 1)))
    }))

; `chanRecv`, waiting at most `nanos` nanoseconds for a word. `Ok (Some
; w)` the oldest word; `Ok None` the end of the stream (closed and
; drained); `Err` with code `sysTimedOut` when the time ran out with the
; ring still empty and open - the defined answer on timeout, which
; takes nothing out of the ring. A non-positive `nanos` is one look.
(pub :: chanRecvTimeout (-> Chan Int (Result (Option Int) Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (chanRecvTimeout c nanos)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (b (chanScratch nanos))
    (mut state 0)
    (mut got 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        {
          (chanLock ch)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (< head tail)
              {
                (set got (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap))))
                (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
                (chanBump ch)
                (chanUnlock ch)
                (chanNotify ch)
                (set state 1)
              }
              (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
                {
                  (chanUnlock ch)
                  (set state 2)
                }
                (if (<= (chanTimeLeft b) 0)
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch)
                    (set state 3)
                  }
                  (let (
                    (seen (chanPark ch))
                    (slice (chanSlice b))
                  )
                    {
                      (chanUnlock ch)
                      (chanTimeSpent b (chanSleepFor ch seen slice) slice)
                    })))))
        })
      (chanScratchDone b)
      (if (== state 1)
        (Ok (Some got))
        (if (== state 2)
          (Ok None)
          (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "chanRecvTimeout: timed out"))))
    }))

; Send without waiting: `True` if `v` went into the ring, `False` if it
; did not - full or closed, which `chanClosed` tells apart, as it does
; for `chanTryRecv`. A `Bool` rather than a three-way `Int`: a -1 for
; "closed" is the sentinel convention the error model is migrating away
; from (`tests/compat/verify-compat.py`).
(pub :: chanTrySend (-> Chan Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanTrySend c v)
  (let ((ch (chanAt c)))
    {
      (chanLock ch)
      (let (
        (head (chanGet ch 3))
        (tail (chanGet ch 4))
        (cap (chanGet ch 5))
      )
        (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
          {
            (chanUnlock ch)
            false
          }
          (if (< (- tail head) cap)
            {
              (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
              (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
              (chanBump ch)
              (chanUnlock ch)
              (chanNotify ch)
              true
            }
            {
              (chanUnlock ch)
              false
            })))
    }))

; Receive without waiting: the oldest word, or `None` when there is
; none right now - empty, whether or not it is closed; `chanClosed`
; tells the two apart.
(pub :: chanTryRecv (-> Chan (Option Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanTryRecv c)
  (let ((ch (chanAt c)))
    {
      (chanLock ch)
      (let (
        (head (chanGet ch 3))
        (tail (chanGet ch 4))
        (cap (chanGet ch 5))
      )
        (if (< head tail)
          (let ((v (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap)))))
            {
              (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
              (chanBump ch)
              (chanUnlock ch)
              (chanNotify ch)
              (Some v)
            })
          {
            (chanUnlock ch)
            None
          }))
    }))

; End the stream. Idempotent. Every waiter wakes: a sender to be
; refused, a receiver to drain and then see `None`.
(pub :: chanClose (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanClose c)
  (let ((ch (chanAt c)))
    {
      (chanLock ch)
      (chanPut ch 6 1)
      (chanBump ch)
      (chanUnlock ch)
      (chanNotify ch)
      0
    }))

(pub :: chanClosed (-> Chan Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanClosed c)
  (let ((ch (chanAt c)))
    {
      (chanLock ch)
      (let ((c (chanGet ch 6)))
        {
          (chanUnlock ch)
          (== c 1)
        })
    }))

; Words in the ring now.
(pub :: chanLen (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanLen c)
  (let ((ch (chanAt c)))
    {
      (chanLock ch)
      (let ((n (- (chanGet ch 4) (chanGet ch 3))))
        {
          (chanUnlock ch)
          n
        })
    }))

(pub :: chanCap (-> Chan Int))
(pub fn (chanCap c)
  (chanGet (chanAt c) 5))

; Unmap the channel. Only once no binding can still reach it - after
; the `parallel` form that used it (the module header's obligation).
; The handle is retired before the ring is unmapped, so every call made
; after this one - a second `chanFree` included - traps with status 85.
(pub :: chanFree (-> Chan (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanFree c)
  (let ((ch (chanRetire c)))
    (sysUnmapShared ch (chanGet ch 7))))
